      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Given that $y=\\sqrt{4x+3},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=4x+3.$ Then $y=\\sqrt{u}={u}^{1/2}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{-1/2}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot (4) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{u}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4x+3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
