      ":question/problem": "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{-2x+12}}.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=-2x+12.$ Then $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}={u}^{-1/2}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=-\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(-2x+12{)}^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
